Silt-Cadence
Ingredients: ZOUK + AFROBEAT + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + Guitar  
Creative Catalyst: It emerged from trans-Saharan merchant caravans that traded dried dates and salt for salvaged 1980s synthesizers left behind at coastal shipping ports.

Silt-Cadence is the sound of late-night oasis tea stalls and sun-faded tape decks: the swaying, pelvic, two-step bounce of French Antillean zouk-love tangled with the intricate, interlocking clean-guitar ostinatos and polyrhythmic percussion of Afrobeat, all bathed in the warm, dust-saturated intimacy of home-recorded lo-fi.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 92–104 BPM. A languid, hips-first Antillean "chabine" pocket layered beneath subtle Afrobeat shekere and clacking woodblocks, dragging slightly behind the beat for an unhurried, cozy head-nod feel.
* Harmonic Palette: Sun-baked major 7ths, Dorian guitar vamps, and suspended 4th chords; breezy West African highlife melodic contours melting into plush, melancholic French-Caribbean pop progressions.
* Production Signatures: 
  1. Sun-Bake Saturation: Guitars and polyphonic synth pads run hot through portable 4-track cassette recorders repeatedly exposed to desert heat, yielding a distinctively soft, warped wow-and-flutter.
  2. Damped Twin Guitars: Two Nile Rodgers-style clean electric guitars—one picking a circular Afrobeat counter-melody, the other palm-muted with foam under the bridge for a dry, woody, tick-tock rhythm.
  3. Dust-Crackle Gate: Ambient field recordings of desert wind, clinking tea glasses, and generator hum used as rhythmic side-chain triggers that gently compress the mix.
* Dynamics: Tracks rarely explode; they drift and breathe like desert heat shimmers, building through the gradual addition of layered hand percussion, murmured bilingual harmonies, and shimmering synth filigree before dissolving into an intimate solo guitar loop.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre coalesced at the remote refueling halts along the Mauritanian railway corridor between Nouadhibou and Zouérat. Saharan caravan traders moving north began acquiring discarded Japanese synthesizers (Yamaha DX-series, Casio CZs, and Roland grooveboxes) offloaded from cargo ships in the Gulf of Guinea. Lacking stable grid power, young musicians plugged these weather-beaten, re-soldered keyboards into 12-volt truck batteries alongside hollow-body electric guitars. By nightfall at the bivouacs, as the bitter green mint tea brewed over coals, players slowed down the kinetic drive of Fela Kuti and the swagger of Kassav' into a relaxed, hearth-side lullaby. Fashion in the scene mirrors the music’s trade-route pedigree: voluminous indigo-dyed Tuareg tagelmust turbans draped over vintage French work jackets, worn-out tennis shoes, and battery belts rigged with soldering irons and 3.5mm headphone jacks.

Typical Instruments: Palm-muted Fender Stratocaster (dead flatwound strings), hollow-body electric guitar, salvaged Yamaha DX7 (producing dust-warped FM electric pianos), Roland Juno-106, acoustic talking drum (loosely tuned and dampened with felt), shekere, calabash, and a vintage Roland TR-606 running through a battered cassette deck.

For Fans Of: Touré Kunda, Khruangbin, Kassav', Tony Allen (specifically his low-tempo collaborations).

