Track 2: Fatimata at the Switchyard
- Story & Context: In the sweltering corrugated-tin tower overlooking the Zouérat sidings, rail dispatcher Fatimata Diallo spent twilight soldering a salvaged Casio organ while the iron rails clicked and cooled below. Over glasses of foam-topped tea, she confessed to the visiting keyboardist that she was systematically forging the railway timetable manifests, delaying the departing freight trains so the caravan's cache of rescued Japanese synthesizers could clear customs unnoticed. Her tired voice was recorded directly through a battered dynamic microphone onto a high-bias cassette tape, wrapped inside circular, warm highlife guitar picking and a pillowy zouk kick. Just as the desert chill settled against the window glass, she whispered the confession that became the song's signature hook: "I changed the departure board so you could finish the song."
