Track 3: Kouté le Convoi
- Story & Context: Decades after asphalt roads bypassed the northern tracks, nomadic herders at the Kilometer 460 water tower began reporting that an abandoned line of rusted hopper wagons was playing melodies without human presence. The myth claims that a synthesizer merchant, stranded during the legendary sandstorms of 1991, left four analog sound generators hardwired to the iron frame of wagon twenty-two, powered by an old solar panel. Whenever the dry Saharan wind blows through the car's corrugated louvers and rattles the rusted suspension, a warm, unmistakable zouk-afrobeat cadence hums across the plains like a ghost band in permanent half-time. Travelers still pull their camels up beside the track to sleep in the resonance, listening to the melodic metal rattle that echoes the chorus line: "Even iron rusts into a dancing rhythm."
